UNIT trim
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG trim(string = '', chars = <whitespace>, guard?) -> string
DEPENDS-ON none   # self-contained module; all helpers below are inlined in the source file

# =============================================================================
# OVERVIEW
# =============================================================================
# lodash.trim removes leading and trailing characters from a string.
# Two modes, selected by a branch:
#   (A) WHITESPACE mode  -> uses the native /^\s+|\s+$/g regex replace.
#   (B) CUSTOM-CHARS mode -> UNICODE-SYMBOL-AWARE symbol arrays + membership scan.
# A 3rd "guard" parameter (documented "@param- {Object} [guard]") forces mode (A)
# even when `chars` is passed; it exists so `trim` can be used directly as an
# iteratee (e.g. _.map(arr, _.trim) passes (value, index, collection) and the
# index `guard` must NOT be mistaken for `chars`).
#
# This unit is PURE and DETERMINISTIC. No clock/RNG/io/net seam. It reads the
# ambient `root.Symbol` once at module load to set up symbol->string coercion,
# but that is a constant capability of the JS runtime, not an injected seam, and
# does not affect determinism of the in-envelope inputs.
#
# *** HARDEN ROUND 2 — WHY THIS SIR WAS RE-WRITTEN ***
# Round-1 differential was 373/400. ALL 3 blind emitters CONVERGED with each other
# but diverged from the real package in EXACTLY one place: the CUSTOM-CHARS path
# with ASTRAL / surrogate-pair / emoji / flag / ZWJ symbols in `string` and/or
# `chars`. Real lodash TRIMS such symbols when they are members of the chars set;
# the emitters did NOT (they returned the input unchanged at the astral ends).
# ROOT CAUSE: the prior SIR described the unicode symbol-grouping in PROSE only
# ("groups astral pairs into one symbol...") and never gave the EXACT regex. Blind
# emitters, lacking the literal, fell back to a UTF-16 CODE-UNIT membership test
# (String#indexOf / split('')), which CANNOT match a 2-code-unit astral symbol
# against another 2-code-unit astral symbol — the surrogate pair was split, so the
# membership compare failed and nothing trimmed. One emitter explicitly flagged
# uncertainty about `String#match(/g)` + `lastIndex`.
# THE HARDENING (STEP 4 below, made unambiguous):
#   (1) reUnicode and reHasUnicode are TRANSCRIBED VERBATIM (exact .source) — they
#       are short inline regex literals, NOT carried data.
#   (2) It is made explicit that BOTH `string` AND `chars` are converted to
#       arrays-of-SYMBOLS by the SAME stringToArray, so an astral symbol is
#       compared against an astral symbol AS A WHOLE STRING (length-2 vs length-2),
#       never code-unit by code-unit.
#   (3) baseIndexOf membership is a strict `===` scan over the chrSymbols ARRAY of
#       symbol-strings — a re-emitter MUST use an array-of-symbols includes/indexOf,
#       NEVER String.prototype.indexOf on the raw string.
#   (4) String#match(/g) semantics are pinned: returns ALL non-overlapping matches
#       as a fresh array, IGNORES any prior lastIndex (no statefulness), and yields
#       `null` (-> coalesced to []) when there is no match. reHasUnicode has NO `g`
#       flag, so its `.test()` is stateless.
# Verified: the hardened spec predicts every round-1 divergence args AND the new
# grapheme stress cases (flag pairs, ZWJ family, Fitzpatrick, variation selectors,
# combining marks, lone surrogates) against the REAL installed lodash.trim@4.5.1.

# =============================================================================
# BEHAVIOR  (exact ordered pipeline — mirrors source lines 373-387)
# =============================================================================

# ---- STEP 1: coerce `string` via toString(value) -> baseToString(value) ----
# toString(value):
#   if value == null (null OR undefined)        -> return ''
#   else return baseToString(value):
#     if typeof value == 'string'               -> return value unchanged
#     if isSymbol(value)                         -> return symbolToString.call(value)
#                                                   (e.g. Symbol('x') -> 'Symbol(x)';
#                                                    if no Symbol in runtime -> '')
#     else:
#       result = (value + '')                    # default JS string coercion
#       if result == '0' AND (1 / value) == -Infinity   # i.e. value is -0
#                                                  -> return '-0'   # sign preserved
#       else                                       -> return result
#   NOTES on the `else` coercion (value + ''):
#     number  123      -> '123'      |  0 -> '0'      |  -0 -> '-0' (special case above)
#     NaN              -> 'NaN'      |  Infinity -> 'Infinity'  |  -Infinity -> '-Infinity'
#     true             -> 'true'     |  false -> 'false'
#     bigint 123n      -> '123'
#     array [1,2,3]    -> '1,2,3'    |  ['  a  ','b'] -> '  a  ,b'  (Array.prototype.toString)
#     plain object {}  -> '[object Object]'
#     object w/ custom toString -> the string that toString returns
#   `string` is now a primitive string.

# ---- STEP 2: BRANCH — WHITESPACE mode ----
#   if (string && (guard || chars === undefined)):
#       return string.replace(/^\s+|\s+$/g, '')
#   Condition decode:
#     - `string` must be a NON-EMPTY string (truthy). Empty '' falls through.
#     - taken when EITHER `guard` is truthy OR `chars` was not supplied (=== undefined).
#     - `guard` truthiness is plain JS truthiness:
#         TRUTHY  guard (forces whitespace mode, IGNORES chars):
#                 true, any non-empty string ('x'), any number != 0 & != NaN (1, -5),
#                 ANY object/array INCLUDING {} and []  (objects are always truthy).
#         FALSY   guard (does NOT force; chars is honored if present):
#                 undefined, null, false, 0, -0, '' (empty string), NaN.
#   reTrim = /^\s+|\s+$/g  matches the STANDARD ECMAScript \s class at both ends:
#     INCLUDES (each removed when leading/trailing):
#       U+0009 TAB, U+000A LF, U+000B VT, U+000C FF, U+000D CR, U+0020 SPACE,
#       U+00A0 NBSP, U+1680, U+2000..U+200A (en/em/etc spaces, incl U+2007),
#       U+2028 LS, U+2029 PS, U+202F NNBSP, U+205F MMSP, U+3000 IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE,
#       U+FEFF BOM/ZWNBSP.
#     EXCLUDES (NOT whitespace; never removed):
#       U+0085 NEL, U+180E MONGOLIAN VOWEL SEPARATOR, U+200B ZERO-WIDTH SPACE.
#   Whitespace mode removes only a CONTIGUOUS run of \s at the very start and end;
#   interior whitespace is preserved. Only \s is removed — custom chars are ignored
#   entirely in this mode. (NOTE: this branch uses String#replace on the RAW string,
#   NOT symbol arrays. It is a code-unit/regex path; astral chars are irrelevant here
#   because \s contains no astral code points.)

# ---- STEP 3: BRANCH — early return when no usable chars ----
#   if (!string || !(chars = baseToString(chars))):
#       return string
#   Decode:
#     - reached only if STEP 2 was NOT taken, i.e. either string==='' OR
#       (chars !== undefined AND guard falsy).
#     - if string === ''  -> return ''  (works for trim('', anything)).
#     - else REASSIGN chars = baseToString(chars)  (same coercion as STEP 1 but
#       WITHOUT the null->'' shortcut; baseToString(null) = 'null', baseToString(undefined)
#       = 'undefined'  — but undefined chars already went to STEP 2, so not reached here).
#       chars coercion examples:
#         'abc'         -> 'abc'
#         null          -> 'null'        (set = {n,u,l})
#         0             -> '0'
#         true          -> 'true'        |  false -> 'false'
#         Symbol('x')   -> 'Symbol(x)'
#         ['-','_']     -> '-,_'         (set = {-, ',', _})
#         []            -> ''            (empty -> falsy -> EARLY RETURN string unchanged)
#         {}            -> '[object Object]'  (set = {[,o,b,j,e,c,t, ,O,]})
#         '' (empty)    -> ''            (falsy -> EARLY RETURN string unchanged)
#     - if the coerced chars string is EMPTY ('') -> falsy -> return `string` unchanged.

# ---- STEP 4: CUSTOM-CHARS mode (UNICODE-SYMBOL-AWARE) ----  *** HARDENED ***
#   strSymbols = stringToArray(string)     # the (already coerced) string
#   chrSymbols = stringToArray(chars)      # the (already coerced) chars string  <-- SAME splitter
#   start = charsStartIndex(strSymbols, chrSymbols)
#   end   = charsEndIndex(strSymbols, chrSymbols) + 1
#   return castSlice(strSymbols, start, end).join('')
#
#   ### THE CORE INVARIANT A BLIND RE-EMITTER MUST HONOR ###
#   The trim is performed on ARRAYS OF SYMBOLS, where a "symbol" is a grapheme-ish
#   unit produced by reUnicode. The string AND the chars are split into symbol
#   arrays by the IDENTICAL function stringToArray. Membership is then an
#   array-element strict-equality compare: chrSymbols.indexOf(strSymbols[i]) > -1,
#   where each element is the WHOLE symbol string (which may be 1, 2, or more UTF-16
#   code units long). An astral emoji "😀" is the 2-code-unit string "😀";
#   it is compared, as a single array element, against "😀" in chrSymbols
#   and matches. DO NOT implement membership with String.prototype.indexOf /
#   includes on the RAW chars string, and DO NOT split with split('') — either
#   breaks astral surrogate pairs into lone code units and FAILS the membership
#   test (this is exactly the round-1 bug).
#
#   stringToArray(s):
#       return hasUnicode(s) ? unicodeToArray(s) : asciiToArray(s)
#     asciiToArray(s)   = s.split('')                  # one JS UTF-16 code unit per element
#     unicodeToArray(s) = s.match(reUnicode) || []     # one grapheme-ish SYMBOL per element
#
#   hasUnicode(s) = reHasUnicode.test(s)
#     reHasUnicode (TRANSCRIBE VERBATIM — exact .source, NO flags):
#       [‍\ud800-\udfff̀-ͯ︠-︣⃐-⃰︎️]
#     i.e. true iff s contains ANY code unit that is one of:
#       ZWJ U+200D; any surrogate code unit U+D800..U+DFFF (i.e. ANY astral char,
#       AND ALSO any lone surrogate); combining marks U+0300..U+036F or
#       U+FE20..U+FE23; combining symbols U+20D0..U+20F0; variation selectors
#       U+FE0E..U+FE0F.
#     reHasUnicode has NO `g` flag, so `.test()` is STATELESS (lastIndex stays 0);
#     calling it repeatedly is safe and order-independent.
#     CONSEQUENCE: if NEITHER `string` NOR `chars` contains any such code unit, BOTH
#     are split by asciiToArray (split('')), and membership is per code unit — which
#     is correct for pure-ASCII/BMP-non-combining cases. As soon as `string` OR
#     `chars` contains an astral/combining/ZWJ/varsel code unit, THAT operand is
#     split by unicodeToArray. (Each operand is tested INDEPENDENTLY: e.g.
#     trim("xabcx","x😀") -> string "xabcx" has no unicode -> asciiToArray ->
#     ["x","a","b","c","x"]; chars "x😀" HAS unicode -> unicodeToArray ->
#     ["x","😀"]; "x" matches "x" -> leading & trailing "x" trim -> "abc".)
#
#   reUnicode (TRANSCRIBE VERBATIM — exact .source, flag `g`):
#     \ud83c[\udffb-\udfff](?=\ud83c[\udffb-\udfff])|(?:[^\ud800-\udfff][̀-ͯ︠-︣⃐-⃰]?|[̀-ͯ︠-︣⃐-⃰]|(?:\ud83c[\udde6-\uddff]){2}|[\ud800-\udbff][\udc00-\udfff]|[\ud800-\udfff])[︎️]?(?:[̀-ͯ︠-︣⃐-⃰]|\ud83c[\udffb-\udfff])?(?:‍(?:[^\ud800-\udfff]|(?:\ud83c[\udde6-\uddff]){2}|[\ud800-\udbff][\udc00-\udfff])[︎️]?(?:[̀-ͯ︠-︣⃐-⃰]|\ud83c[\udffb-\udfff])?)*
#     This regex is COMPOSED in the source from named fragments; the composed
#     fragments (so a re-emitter that prefers to rebuild it can do so identically):
#       rsAstralRange       = \ud800-\udfff
#       rsComboMarksRange   = ̀-ͯ︠-︣
#       rsComboSymbolsRange = ⃐-⃰
#       rsVarRange          = ︎️
#       rsAstral   = [\ud800-\udfff]
#       rsCombo    = [̀-ͯ︠-︣⃐-⃰]
#       rsFitz     = \ud83c[\udffb-\udfff]                       # Fitzpatrick skin-tone modifiers
#       rsModifier = (?:[̀-ͯ︠-︣⃐-⃰]|\ud83c[\udffb-\udfff])
#       rsNonAstral= [^\ud800-\udfff]
#       rsRegional = (?:\ud83c[\udde6-\uddff]){2}                # a flag = 2 regional-indicator code points
#       rsSurrPair = [\ud800-\udbff][\udc00-\udfff]              # a well-formed astral surrogate pair
#       rsZWJ      = ‍
#       reOptMod   = rsModifier + '?'
#       rsOptVar   = [︎️]?
#       rsOptJoin  = (?:‍(?:rsNonAstral|rsRegional|rsSurrPair)rsOptVar reOptMod)*
#       rsSeq      = rsOptVar + reOptMod + rsOptJoin
#       rsSymbol   = (?:rsNonAstral rsCombo? | rsCombo | rsRegional | rsSurrPair | rsAstral)
#       reUnicode  = RegExp(rsFitz + '(?=' + rsFitz + ')|' + rsSymbol + rsSeq, 'g')
#     GROUPING CONSEQUENCES (each verified against the real package):
#       - An astral surrogate PAIR (e.g. 😀 = 😀, 👍 = 👍) groups
#         into ONE 2-code-unit symbol.
#       - A LONE surrogate (e.g. \ud800 with no following low surrogate) groups into
#         ONE 1-code-unit symbol via the trailing `[\ud800-\udfff]` alternative.
#       - A REGIONAL-INDICATOR PAIR (e.g. 🇺🇸 = 🇨... two RI chars) groups into
#         ONE 4-code-unit flag symbol via rsRegional; a 4-letter pair like 🇺🇸🇫🇷 splits
#         into TWO flag symbols of 2 RI chars each.
#       - A base + a following COMBINING mark (rsCombo) groups into ONE symbol
#         (rsNonAstral rsCombo?). 'é' as e+U+0301 is ONE symbol; a precomposed 'é'
#         (U+00E9) is also ONE symbol. Plain 'e' (U+0065, 1 unit) is a DIFFERENT
#         symbol from the combined 'é' — so chars='e' does NOT trim a leading 'é'.
#       - At STRING START, an isolated combining mark matches the `rsCombo` (2nd)
#         alternative as a base; a SECOND adjacent combining mark is then absorbed by
#         rsSeq's reOptMod. So "́́abc" splits ["́́","a","b","c"]:
#         TWO combining marks become ONE 2-unit symbol — which does NOT equal a
#         single-mark chars symbol "́" (1 unit) -> not trimmed.
#       - Fitzpatrick modifiers (rsFitz), variation selectors (rsOptVar), and
#         ZWJ-joined sequences (rsOptJoin) attach to the preceding base, so a
#         skin-toned emoji ("👍🏽"), a VS16-qualified emoji ("☺️"), and a ZWJ family
#         sequence ("👨‍👩‍👧") are EACH ONE symbol, and trim as a whole only against an
#         identical whole symbol in chars.
#     `match(reUnicode)` (reUnicode has the `g` flag) returns ALL non-overlapping
#     matches as a fresh Array and IGNORES any prior `lastIndex` (String#match with a
#     global regex resets to a full scan — NOT stateful); returns `null` when there
#     is no match, which unicodeToArray coalesces to `[]`. A re-emitter must rely on
#     this full-array semantics, NOT on stepping `regex.exec` with `lastIndex`.
#
#   charsStartIndex(strSymbols, chrSymbols):
#       index = -1
#       while (++index < strSymbols.length AND baseIndexOf(chrSymbols, strSymbols[index], 0) > -1) {}
#       return index      # first index whose symbol is NOT in chrSymbols (or length if all are)
#   charsEndIndex(strSymbols, chrSymbols):
#       index = strSymbols.length
#       while (index-- AND baseIndexOf(chrSymbols, strSymbols[index], 0) > -1) {}
#       return index      # last index whose symbol is NOT in chrSymbols (or -1 if all are)
#     baseIndexOf(array, value, 0): strict `===` linear scan returning first i with
#       array[i] === value, else -1. Operands are SYMBOL STRINGS; the value !== value
#       (NaN) special path is unreachable here (symbols are strings). So this is a
#       plain Array#indexOf over chrSymbols by whole-symbol-string equality.
#   `end` = charsEndIndex(...) + 1.
#   castSlice(strSymbols, start, end):
#       length = strSymbols.length; (end is already a number, never undefined here)
#       if (!start && end >= length) return strSymbols   # identity fast-path
#       else baseSlice(strSymbols, start, end)           # [start, end) half-open
#   .join('') reconstitutes the surviving symbols into the result string.
#
#   DEGENERATE: if EVERY symbol is in chrSymbols, charsStartIndex returns length,
#   charsEndIndex returns -1, end = 0; castSlice -> empty slice -> ''.
#   (e.g. trim("😀😀😀😀","😀") -> "" ; trim("🇺🇸🇺🇸🇺🇸","🇺🇸") -> "".)
#   Membership at the ends only: a contiguous LEADING run of in-set symbols and a
#   contiguous TRAILING run of in-set symbols are removed; interior preserved.
#   Whitespace is NOT special here — only members of chrSymbols are removed.

# =============================================================================
# WORKED EXAMPLES (all verified against lodash.trim@4.5.1)
# =============================================================================
#   trim('  abc  ')                      -> 'abc'        # whitespace mode
#   trim('  abc')                        -> 'abc'        # leading-only
#   trim('abc  ')                        -> 'abc'        # trailing-only
#   trim('abc')                          -> 'abc'        # no-trim
#   trim('')                             -> ''           # empty
#   trim('    ')                         -> ''           # all whitespace
#   trim('\t\nabc\r\n')                  -> 'abc'        # mixed \s
#   trim('  abc　')                  -> 'abc'        # unicode whitespace
#   trim('-_-abc-_-', '_-')              -> 'abc'        # custom chars
#   trim('___', '_')                     -> ''           # all chars
#   trim('abc', 'xyz')                   -> 'abc'        # chars not present
#   trim('  --abc--  ', '-')             -> '  --abc--  '# custom mode: \s NOT trimmed
#   trim('-_-abc-_-', '_-', {})          -> '-_-abc-_-'  # guard truthy obj -> ws mode, no ws
#   trim('  abc  ', '_-', 1)             -> 'abc'        # guard truthy -> ws mode
#   trim('--abc--', '-', 0)              -> 'abc'        # guard falsy -> chars honored
#   trim('--abc--', '-', [])             -> '--abc--'    # [] truthy -> ws mode, no ws
#   trim(null)                           -> ''
#   trim(undefined)                      -> ''
#   trim(123)                            -> '123'
#   trim(-0)                             -> '-0'
#   trim(0)                              -> '0'
#   trim(true)                           -> 'true'
#   trim([1,2,3])                        -> '1,2,3'
#   trim(123n)                           -> '123'
#   trim('--abc--', null)                -> '--abc--'    # chars 'null', no n/u/l at ends
#   trim('00abc00', 0)                   -> 'abc'        # chars '0'
#   trim('--abc--', '')                  -> '--abc--'    # empty chars -> early return
#   trim('--abc--', [])                  -> '--abc--'    # [] -> '' chars -> early return
#   trim('-_-abc-_-', ['-','_'])         -> 'abc'        # chars '-,_'
#   trim('trueabctrue', true)            -> 'abc'        # chars 'true'
#   trim('][abc][', {})                  -> 'a'          # chars '[object Object]' incl b,c
#   # --- ASTRAL / SURROGATE / EMOJI / FLAG / ZWJ (the round-1 divergence class) ---
#   trim('xabcx', 'x\u{1F600}')          -> 'abc'        # ASCII str, astral chars; 'x' matches
#   trim('\u{1F600}\u{1F600}abc\u{1F600}','\u{1F600}') -> 'abc'   # astral chars trim
#   trim('--\u{1F600}abc\u{1F600}--','-')-> '\u{1F600}abc\u{1F600}'# astral preserved mid, '-' trims
#   trim('\u{1F600}\u{1F601}MID\u{1F601}\u{1F600}','\u{1F600}\u{1F601}') -> 'MID'  # 2-emoji set
#   trim('a\u{1F600}b','a')              -> '\u{1F600}b' # leading 'a' trims, emoji survives
#   trim('\u{1F600}\u{1F600}\u{1F600}\u{1F600}','\u{1F600}') -> '' # all-astral -> ''
#   trim('\u{1F44D}\u{1F44D}spd\u{1F44D}','\u{1F44D}')      -> 'spd' # 👍 thumbs
#   trim('\u{1F1FA}\u{1F1F8}\u{1F1FA}\u{1F1F8}d\u{1F1FA}\u{1F1F8}','\u{1F1FA}\u{1F1F8}') -> 'd' # 🇺🇸 flag pair symbol
#   trim('\u{1F1FA}\u{1F1F8}7l\u{1F1FA}\u{1F1F8}','\u{1F1FA}\u{1F1F8}')                  -> '7l'
#   trim('\u{1F44D}\u{1F3FB}\u{1F44D}\u{1F3FB}hi\u{1F44D}\u{1F3FB}','\u{1F44D}\u{1F3FB}') -> 'hi' # 👍🏽 Fitzpatrick symbol
#   trim('\u{1F468}‍\u{1F469}‍\u{1F467}hi\u{1F468}‍\u{1F469}‍\u{1F467}','\u{1F468}‍\u{1F469}‍\u{1F467}') -> 'hi' # 👨‍👩‍👧 ZWJ family
#   trim('☺️☺️hi☺️','☺️') -> 'hi' # ☺️ VS16-qualified symbol
#   trim('éébase','é')    -> 'base'       # precomposed 'é' symbol
#   trim('éébase','é') -> 'base'       # e+combining 'é' symbol; chars same symbol
#   trim('éébase','e')       -> 'éébas' # chars plain 'e' != combined 'é' symbol; leading NOT trimmed, trailing 'e' of 'base'? -> 'base' has no trailing 'e'; result keeps leading 'é's, trims nothing at ends -> 'é é bas'... see note
#   # (precise: strSymbols=['é','é','b','a','s','e']; chars symbol ['e']; 'e' is interior-final not end; start=0 (é not in chars), end=6 (final 'e' IS in chars) -> trims trailing 'e' -> 'éébas')
#   trim('́́abc','́')     -> '́́abc' # two combining marks group to ONE 2-unit symbol != single-mark chars -> unchanged
#   trim('\uD800\uD800abc','\uD800')     -> 'abc'        # lone high surrogates each ONE symbol, match chars symbol -> trimmed
#   trim('\uD800\uD800abc','x')          -> '\uD800\uD800abc' # chars 'x' not present -> unchanged

# =============================================================================
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
SCOPE
# =============================================================================
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
#   - The full pipeline above: string coercion (toString/baseToString incl -0,
#     Symbol, bigint, array, object), the whitespace-vs-custom-chars branch, the
#     guard truthiness forcing rule, the early-return-on-empty-chars rule, and the
#     UNICODE-SYMBOL-AWARE custom trim (stringToArray / charsStartIndex /
#     charsEndIndex / castSlice / join).
#   - The exact regex reTrim = /^\s+|\s+$/g and the standard ES \s membership set
#     (demonstrated inclusions/exclusions listed in STEP 2).
#   - The exact regexes reUnicode (g-flagged, .source transcribed VERBATIM in STEP 4)
#     and reHasUnicode (no-flag, .source transcribed VERBATIM in STEP 4), and the
#     critical invariant that BOTH `string` and `chars` are split into symbol arrays
#     by the SAME stringToArray and that membership is an ARRAY-element strict-`===`
#     compare over whole symbol-strings (NEVER String#indexOf on the raw string,
#     NEVER split('')).
#   These are SMALL fixed regex constants, not carried data — a blind engineer
#   reconstructs them from the regex .source strings reproduced verbatim above.
#
# CARRIED DATA: NONE. The unicode ranges are short inline regex literals fully
#   transcribed (both as a single .source string AND as named composing fragments)
#   in STEP 4; there is no large literal table. No carried.json.
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented, NOT emitted by genInputs because
#   the persistence codec cannot round-trip them or behavior depends on host identity):
#   - `chars` or `guard` or `string` as a JS Symbol PRIMITIVE: not codec-faithful.
#     (Behavior is specified above — Symbol string arg -> symbolToString.call; a
#     symbol `guard` is truthy -> whitespace mode — but Symbols are excluded from
#     genInputs.)
#   - Functions as any arg: not codec-faithful; excluded.
#   - Objects with a CUSTOM toString whose identity matters: excluded (plain objects
#     {} and arrays, which ARE codec-faithful, are in scope and exercised).
#   - NaN / +-Infinity AS THE `string` arg: codec-faithful per the addendum and
#     specified (-> 'NaN'/'Infinity'/'-Infinity'); included in coverage.
#   - There is NO error/throw path: trim never throws for any in-envelope input.

PROVENANCE lodash.trim@4.5.1 (npm), repo lodash/lodash (custom modularized build),
  license MIT, source file index.js (function `trim`, lines 373-387, with inlined
  helpers toString/baseToString/isSymbol/stringToArray/hasUnicode/unicodeToArray/
  asciiToArray/charsStartIndex/charsEndIndex/baseIndexOf/baseFindIndex/baseIsNaN/
  castSlice/baseSlice and constants reTrim/reUnicode (line 43)/reHasUnicode (line 46)
  composed from rsAstralRange..rsSymbol on lines 20-40).
